    match node {
        TrieElement::Branch(node) => {
            hash_builder.add_branch(node.key, node.value, node.children_are_in_trie);
        }
        TrieElement::Leaf(hashed_address, account) => {
            let trie_account = account.into_trie_account(storage_root);
            trie_account.encode(&mut account_rlp);
            hash_builder.add_leaf(Nibbles::unpack(hashed_address), &account_rlp);
        }
    }
}
let root = hash_builder.root();
```

## ITEM 17: CHK-0035 (PROP-0002)
Title: "Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Does patricialize terminate for all valid inputs?"
Severity: Critical

The patricialize function is in `alloy_trie`, not in reth directly. Reth uses `HashBuilder` which internally builds the trie structure. The `HashBuilder` processes keys in sorted order, advancing through the key space. Since keys have finite length (64 nibbles for secured/hashed keys), the iteration always terminates.

## ITEM 18: CHK-0036 (PROP-0003)
Title: "Are default values excluded from trie._data (no ghost entries)?"
Severity: Critical

In Reth's implementation, this maps to how storage slots are handled. When a storage slot is set to zero, it should be removed from the trie. Reth's storage root computation uses `HashedStorageCursor` which iterates over non-zero storage values. The EVM (revm) handles storage slot deletion when values are set to zero. The `StorageRoot::calculate()` method only processes entries returned by the cursor, which excludes zeroed slots.

## ITEM 19: CHK-0037 (PROP-0004)
Title: "Does trie_get return the exact value set by trie_set?"
Severity: Critical

This is about Reth's storage/state access. Reth uses a database layer (MDBX) to store state. The `HashedCursorFactory` provides cursors that read from the database. Values written to the database are read back exactly. This is guaranteed by the database layer's ACID properties and RLP encoding/decoding roundtrip.

