## ITEM 25: CHK-0043 (PROP-0011)
Title: "Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Is RLP encoding deterministic and canonical?"
Severity: Critical

RLP encoding in Reth uses `alloy_rlp` library which implements canonical RLP encoding per the Ethereum specification. The encoding is deterministic - same input always produces same output. The library is shared across multiple Ethereum clients and extensively tested.

Code scope: Uses alloy_rlp::Encodable trait throughout. For trie accounts:
```rust
// crates/trie/common/src/constants.rs
pub const TRIE_ACCOUNT_RLP_MAX_SIZE: usize = 110;
// 2 (header) + 4 * 1 (field lens) + 8 (nonce) + 32 * 3 (balance, storage root, code hash)
```

## ITEM 26: CHK-0044 (PROP-0012)
Title: "Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Is keccak256 deterministic and collision-resistant?"
Severity: Critical

Code from crates/trie/common/src/key.rs:
```rust
pub struct KeccakKeyHasher;
impl KeyHasher for KeccakKeyHasher {
    fn hash_key<T: AsRef<[u8]>>(bytes: T) -> B256 { keccak256(bytes) }
}
```

keccak256 is imported from `alloy_primitives` which uses a well-tested, standard Keccak-256 implementation. It is deterministic and cryptographically secure.

## ITEM 27: CHK-0045 (PROP-0013)
Title: "Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Is keccak256 collision resistance maintained?"
Severity: Critical

This is a cryptographic property of the Keccak-256 algorithm itself (128-bit collision resistance). No code-level vulnerability exists - this is about the mathematical properties of the hash function. The implementation uses the standard Keccak-256 from the `alloy_primitives` crate.

## ITEM 28: CHK-0046 (PROP-0014)
Title: "Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Is encode_node type dispatch correct?"
Severity: High

In Reth's implementation, node encoding is handled by Rust's type system and trait implementations. Each type (Account, U256, Bytes) implements `alloy_rlp::Encodable`. The type dispatch is guaranteed at compile time by Rust's type system, not by runtime isinstance checks. The `into_trie_account()` method on `Account` produces a `TrieAccount` which is then RLP-encoded.

Code from crates/trie/trie/src/trie.rs:
```rust
let trie_account = account.into_trie_account(storage_root);
trie_account.encode(&mut self.account_rlp as &mut dyn BufMut);
hash_builder.add_leaf(Nibbles::unpack(hashed_address), &self.account_rlp);
```

For storage values:
```rust
hash_builder.add_leaf(Nibbles::unpack(hashed_slot), alloy_rlp::encode_fixed_size(&value).as_ref());
```

## ITEM 29: CHK-0047 (PROP-0015)
Title: "Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Is previous_trie fallback encoding compatible?"
Severity: High

Reth does not use a "previous_trie" fallback pattern like the Python Ethereum spec. Instead, encoding is done directly through RLP. The encoding is the same regardless of fork - Account always encodes as (nonce, balance, storage_root, code_hash) via TrieAccount. Cross-fork compatibility is handled by chain spec, not by encoding fallbacks.

## ITEM 30: CHK-0048 (PROP-0016)
Title: "Does copy_trie produce a fully independent copy?"
Severity: High

Reth doesn't have a direct "copy_trie" operation like the Python spec. State snapshots/reverts are handled by the database layer (MDBX) and the in-memory state. The `CachedStateProvider` and journaled state in revm handle transaction-level reverts. Each transaction execution produces independent state changes that can be reverted without affecting other state.

## ITEM 31: CHK-0049 (PROP-0018)
Title: "Does trie_set correctly delete keys when value equals default?"
Severity: High

In Reth, storage deletion is handled by the EVM execution layer. When SSTORE sets a value to zero, the revm marks the slot for deletion. The `HashedPostState` captures these changes and the trie computation excludes deleted slots. The storage cursor's `is_storage_empty()` check and iteration logic correctly handle zero-value slots.

## ITEM 32: CHK-0050 (PROP-0019)
Title: "Is trie_set a no-op when deleting non-existent key?"
Severity: Medium

When the EVM's SSTORE instruction sets a non-existent storage slot to zero, the gas cost correctly reflects a no-op (no state change). The trie computation skips non-existent keys naturally since they don't appear in the hashed cursor iteration. The `HashedPostState` won't include unchanged entries.

## ITEM 33: CHK-0051 (PROP-0020)
Title: "Does trie_get return trie.default for unset keys?"
Severity: High

In Reth's EVM execution, reading an unset storage slot returns U256::ZERO (the default). This is handled by revm's state implementation. The SLOAD instruction returns zero for non-existent slots. This is guaranteed by the EVM specification and the revm implementation.

## ITEM 34: CHK-0052 (PROP-0023)
Title: "Are nibble values validated to be in range [0, 15]?"
Severity: Medium

Code from crates/trie/common/src/nibbles.rs:
```rust
pub struct StoredNibbles(pub Nibbles);
// Nibbles from nybbles crate
```

Nibbles are created via `Nibbles::unpack()` which converts bytes to nibbles by splitting each byte into two 4-bit values (high nibble and low nibble). Since each nibble is derived from 4 bits of a byte, values are always in [0, 15] by construction. The bit masking in unpack guarantees this.

## ITEM 35: CHK-0053 (PROP-0024)
Title: "Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Is compact encoding header byte correct?"
Severity: Critical

Compact encoding (hex prefix encoding) is implemented in the `alloy_trie`/`nybbles` libraries. The encoding follows the Ethereum spec:
- Even-length leaf: 0x20
- Odd-length leaf: 0x3X (where X is first nibble)
- Even-length extension: 0x00
- Odd-length extension: 0x1X

This is handled by the HashBuilder and is well-tested in the alloy-trie library.

